-- title:
--   Kernel of PicXtoPicU generated by the Cᵢ
-- statement:
--   Let $X$ be an integral, locally Noetherian scheme and $U$ an open subscheme of $X$. Let $\iota$ be a finite index type and $C : \iota \to$ `X.IdealSheafData` a family of quasi-coherent ideals, each invertible in the sense that every point of $X$ has an affine open neighbourhood of the form $X.\mathrm{basicOpen}\,f$ on which the ideal is generated by a single non-zero-divisor of $\Gamma(X, X.\mathrm{affineBasicOpen}\,f)$. Assume: the support of each $C_i$ is disjoint from $U$; points $\eta_i \notin U$ are given such that for each $i$ there is an affine open $V$ containing $\eta_i$ whose germ map carries $(C_i).\mathrm{ideal}\,V$ onto the maximal ideal of the stalk $\mathcal O_{X,\eta_i}$ and carries $(C_j).\mathrm{ideal}\,V$ onto the unit ideal for every $j \neq i$; every $x \notin U$ with $\operatorname{ringKrullDim}\mathcal O_{X,x} \le 1$ equals some $\eta_i$; and for every $x \notin U$ each height-one prime of $\mathcal O_{X,x}$ is principal. Let $L$ be a module over $X$ that is invertible, i.e. each point has an open neighbourhood $W$ with the restriction of $L$ to $W$ isomorphic to the unit sheaf, and let $s : \mathbf 1 \to L$ be a section whose zero-scheme ideal $\mathrm{zeroSchemeIdeal}\,s$ (the infimum of those ideal sheaf data whose ideals dominate the span of the coefficients of $s$ on all affine opens) has support disjoint from $U$. Then there are natural numbers $n_i$ and an isomorphism $L \cong \bigl(\prod_i C_i^{\,n_i}\bigr).\mathrm{invModule}$, the dual of the module of the product ideal.
--
--   This is the statement, in divisor-theoretic form, that the kernel of the restriction map $\operatorname{Pic}X \to \operatorname{Pic}U$ is generated by the classes of the components $C_i$ of the complement, in the setting of a model over a discrete valuation ring with $U$ its generic fibre: a line bundle carrying a section vanishing only outside $U$ is $\mathcal O_X(\sum_i n_i C_i)$. It is used in the analysis of line bundles on a Deligne–Rapoport model of a modular curve, where the hypotheses on $\eta_i$ and on height-one primes record that the $C_i$ are all the components of the special fibre and that the relevant local rings are factorial in codimension one.
